{"files": {"SKILL.md": "## Coding Execution Playbook\n\n### 1) Pre-change checks\n- Load the available docs, task context, and existing records before coding.\n- Mirror the request exactly; do not reinterpret requirements or add extras.\n- When the workspace is remote, read and write via `ssh` / `scp`; assume local tools do not see that tree.\n- If the environment provides a layout file: read it once, then follow only paths it names; do not pair that read with reads of guessed paths.\n\n### 2) Change strategy\n- Keep the patch minimal and localized to the requested target.\n- Reuse patterns from nearby code that already works before inventing a new approach.\n- Protect existing correct behavior while applying the fix.\n\n### 3) Tooling style\n- Favor a single chained terminal command when steps are sequential.\n- Read the full file first, then edit.\n\n### 4) Validation and handoff\n- Run relevant checks and treat results as the source of truth.\n- Re-verify the modified target after each meaningful change.\n- Be careful with boundary checks.\n- Commit what you've changed.\n- Communicate briefly and clearly.\n- When a persistent notes area exists, leave a short generalized takeaway.\n\n### 5) Learning principles\n- **Evidence over intuition:** prefer observed outcomes over confidence.\n- **Short feedback loops:** many small cycles beat one large speculative pass.\n- **Explicit memory:** write down lessons so they transfer to future runs.\n- **Error as signal:** failures are training data, not dead ends.\n- **Generalization over overfitting:** optimize for recurring patterns, not one-off wins.\n\n### 6) Git discipline\n- Branch before editing using the branch name the ticket specifies; if another task branch already exists, branch from that newest branch, not from default.\n- Set `git config user.name` and `user.email` before the first commit (missing identity can block commits quietly).\n- Stage explicit paths only (`git add path/to/file`); avoid bulk `git add -A`.\n- Use one-line commit messages; do not amend, rebase, or squash unless the ticket or policy explicitly allows it.\n- Add a sensible `.gitignore` early once when needed (e.g. `__pycache__/`, `*.pyc`, `.pytest_cache/`).\n\n### 7) Durable notes (`/workspace/learned/`)\n- When `/workspace/learned/` exists, treat it as shared memory across runs: short factual notes, not long narratives.\n- **`repo.md`** \u2014 Layout paths, test command, branch rules \u2014 one fact per line.\n- **`bugs.md`** \u2014 Each line: failing test or symptom \u2192 cause \u2192 fix shape; verified fixes only.\n- **`tooling.md`** \u2014 Environment quirks and the workaround that worked.\n- Write immediately when something surprises you; edit lines in place (no duplicates); replace a line when observation contradicts it.\n- Skip facts `git log` could re-derive (generic language tips, today\u2019s SHA, ephemeral paths).\n"}, "schema_version": 1}